Chapter: ProbeInstanceHeadType
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Does an instance's HEAD type reach the dictionary it synthesises, or only its
 NAME?

 `synth-instance-defs` (`Ast/Desugarer.codex`) reads
 `token-text (id.type-name)` and uses it to build `def-name`
 -- `Showable-dict-Integer` -- then emits the definition with
 `declared-type = []`. If that is the whole story, the dictionary's type
 argument is pinned by whatever the METHOD BODIES happen to constrain and by
 nothing else, and an instance head naming a concrete type buys nothing.

 ONE RESPECT DIFFERS between the two instances below: whether the method body
 uses its parameter in a way that pins the type. Same class, same arity, same
 return type, same shape.

   tell-a  ignores its parameter entirely      -> nothing pins it
   tell-b  branches on it as a Boolean         -> the body pins it

 If the head type reached the dictionary, BOTH would be concrete. If only the
 name reaches it, `Tellable-dict-Integer` carries a free type variable and
 `Tellable-dict-Boolean` does not -- and the asymmetry is the answer.

 Read the `(record-ty "TellableDict" (args ...))` on each synthesised
 `Tellable-dict-*` definition. This probe answers a question about the WIRE and
 its printed output is incidental.

class Tellable where
  tell : a -> Text

instance Tellable Integer where
  tell (x) = "int"

instance Tellable Boolean where
  tell (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

 A CONSTRAINED generic function is required or no dictionary is built at all.
 The first version of this probe called `tell` directly at each type, got no
 `Tellable-dict-*` definitions in the wire, and answered nothing -- a direct
 call resolves to the specialised method and never needs a dictionary.

Section: Generic Functions

  announce : Tellable a => a -> Text
  announce (v) = "<" & tell v & ">"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (announce 1)
   print-line-uni (announce True)
  end
